1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,921
What if one day your body can no longer take you where you want to go?

2
00:00:04,908 --> 00:00:08,005
What if you wake up and there is nowhere you want to go anyway?

3
00:00:08,985 --> 00:00:13,147
And when so much of the person you used to be is gone, what is still there?

4
00:00:18,836 --> 00:00:20,541
When I think about growing old,

5
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:22,785
I don't have to imagine very much.

6
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:25,860
My grandmother had a stroke and spent

7
00:00:25,874 --> 00:00:28,076
more than ten years in a nursing home.

8
00:00:29,056 --> 00:00:30,817
She lost the ability to speak,

9
00:00:31,073 --> 00:00:32,295
and much of her ability

10
00:00:32,309 --> 00:00:33,658
to communicate with us.

11
00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:36,243
After my father retired,

12
00:00:36,499 --> 00:00:38,630
his world gradually became smaller.

13
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:42,706
He spent most of his time at home watching television.

14
00:00:43,687 --> 00:00:47,053
Health problems eventually made even going out difficult.

15
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:51,655
My mother began showing signs of dementia after an accident.

16
00:00:52,635 --> 00:00:55,547
On days when she doesn't go to a day care center,

17
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,246
she can spend hours simply sitting in a chair.

18
00:00:59,948 --> 00:01:00,942
When I look at them,

19
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:03,045
my first reaction is simple:

20
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:05,779
I don't want to grow old like that.

21
00:01:06,759 --> 00:01:09,216
But then comes a more uncomfortable thought.

22
00:01:10,196 --> 00:01:11,915
What if I don't get to choose?

23
00:01:13,604 --> 00:01:14,783
I can exercise,

24
00:01:14,953 --> 00:01:15,948
watch what I eat,

25
00:01:16,189 --> 00:01:17,027
and save enough

26
00:01:17,042 --> 00:01:18,348
money for retirement.

27
00:01:19,335 --> 00:01:20,315
But none of these can

28
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:21,821
guarantee what kind of old

29
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:23,185
person I will become.

30
00:01:24,165 --> 00:01:26,210
I may lose the ability to travel.

31
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:30,017
I may need help getting through an ordinary day.

32
00:01:30,997 --> 00:01:33,455
I may no longer be able to read for hours

33
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:35,855
or sit in front of a laptop working on some

34
00:01:35,869 --> 00:01:38,057
project that nobody asked me to do.

35
00:01:39,037 --> 00:01:40,358
All of that scares me.

36
00:01:42,044 --> 00:01:44,601
But what scares me most is something simpler:

37
00:01:45,588 --> 00:01:48,656
Waking up in the morning and having nothing I want to do.

38
00:01:50,348 --> 00:01:53,885
Right now, there are always things I want to experience,

39
00:01:54,084 --> 00:01:56,768
understand, make, or write down.

40
00:01:57,755 --> 00:01:59,886
I want to go somewhere I haven't been,

41
00:02:00,142 --> 00:02:01,477
see how people live there,

42
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:02,585
then come back,

43
00:02:02,883 --> 00:02:03,963
open my laptop,

44
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:05,042
and work on one

45
00:02:05,057 --> 00:02:06,164
of my projects.

46
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:08,395
Something comes in.

47
00:02:09,375 --> 00:02:10,156
And I still have

48
00:02:10,170 --> 00:02:10,937
something I want

49
00:02:10,951 --> 00:02:12,173
to send back out.

50
00:02:13,868 --> 00:02:17,348
Maybe this is one thing we underestimate about retirement.

51
00:02:18,335 --> 00:02:19,926
Work gives us more than money.

52
00:02:20,906 --> 00:02:22,057
It gives us structure,

53
00:02:22,241 --> 00:02:23,434
problems to solve,

54
00:02:23,662 --> 00:02:24,656
people to talk to,

55
00:02:24,883 --> 00:02:25,778
and things that have

56
00:02:25,792 --> 00:02:26,645
to be finished.

57
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:29,670
Then one day, it stops.

58
00:02:31,364 --> 00:02:34,034
I can tell myself I will have plenty to do.

59
00:02:35,021 --> 00:02:36,982
I already have too many projects

60
00:02:36,996 --> 00:02:39,198
and too many places I want to see.

61
00:02:40,178 --> 00:02:42,663
But what will I want when I am seventy-five?

62
00:02:43,644 --> 00:02:44,752
I don't really know.

63
00:02:46,436 --> 00:02:49,149
There is another part of growing old that bothers me.

64
00:02:50,136 --> 00:02:51,770
I don't want to become a burden.

65
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:54,795
I want my child to care about me.

66
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:57,551
But I don't want his life to revolve

67
00:02:57,565 --> 00:02:59,127
around taking care of me.

68
00:03:00,108 --> 00:03:02,224
I don't need complete independence.

69
00:03:03,204 --> 00:03:04,610
Some help is fine.

70
00:03:05,591 --> 00:03:06,571
What bothers me is

71
00:03:06,585 --> 00:03:07,480
the thought of another

72
00:03:07,494 --> 00:03:08,588
person's life being

73
00:03:08,602 --> 00:03:10,363
consumed by my needs.

74
00:03:12,068 --> 00:03:13,261
And, to be honest,

75
00:03:13,559 --> 00:03:14,639
I am really hoping

76
00:03:14,653 --> 00:03:15,363
we will have good

77
00:03:15,378 --> 00:03:16,940
care robots by then.

78
00:03:17,927 --> 00:03:19,348
If technology and a good

79
00:03:19,362 --> 00:03:20,967
care system can handle more

80
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:22,160
of the day-to-day care,

81
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:23,865
perhaps my family doesn't

82
00:03:23,879 --> 00:03:25,683
have to become my care system.

83
00:03:26,663 --> 00:03:28,637
They can simply remain my family.

84
00:03:30,332 --> 00:03:32,960
So imagine an ordinary Tuesday morning.

85
00:03:33,947 --> 00:03:35,523
I am eighty years old.

86
00:03:36,504 --> 00:03:38,379
There is nowhere I have to be.

87
00:03:39,359 --> 00:03:41,475
Nobody is waiting for my work.

88
00:03:42,455 --> 00:03:44,387
My child has his own life.

89
00:03:45,371 --> 00:03:46,294
What would make me want

90
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:47,444
to get out of bed?

91
00:03:49,124 --> 00:03:50,985
I think I would still like to ask:

92
00:03:51,972 --> 00:03:53,804
How can I live this day well?

93
00:03:54,784 --> 00:03:55,977
And is there something I can

94
00:03:55,992 --> 00:03:57,469
leave behind in the world?

95
00:03:58,449 --> 00:04:00,097
I don't mean anything grand.

96
00:04:01,077 --> 00:04:02,526
Maybe a few strangers

97
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:03,847
read something I wrote,

98
00:04:04,017 --> 00:04:05,296
watch a video I made,

99
00:04:05,523 --> 00:04:06,517
or use some little

100
00:04:06,531 --> 00:04:07,710
project I built.

101
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:10,225
People I will never meet.

102
00:04:11,824 --> 00:04:13,396
For most of my life,

103
00:04:13,397 --> 00:04:15,441
the world has been sending

104
00:04:15,442 --> 00:04:17,722
things into me—books, places,

105
00:04:17,723 --> 00:04:20,160
people, questions, experiences.

106
00:04:20,161 --> 00:04:22,048
I hope I will still have

107
00:04:22,049 --> 00:04:23,857
something to send back.

108
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:25,981
Not because I need to prove

109
00:04:25,982 --> 00:04:27,318
that I am useful.

110
00:04:27,319 --> 00:04:30,464
Just because the conversation between me

111
00:04:30,465 --> 00:04:32,982
and the world is still going on.

112
00:04:34,676 --> 00:04:38,057
But even that may be asking too much of my future self.

113
00:04:39,044 --> 00:04:43,177
Maybe one day I won't be able to write or work on projects anymore.

114
00:04:44,157 --> 00:04:47,595
Maybe I will just sit by a window, listen to music,

115
00:04:47,865 --> 00:04:49,569
eat something I still enjoy,

116
00:04:49,796 --> 00:04:51,870
and talk to someone once in a while.

117
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:54,725
If I am not in constant pain,

118
00:04:54,939 --> 00:04:55,862
perhaps that would be

119
00:04:55,876 --> 00:04:57,240
a pretty good day too.

120
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:02,449
It would be easy to turn aging into another performance target:

121
00:05:02,662 --> 00:05:04,778
stay curious, stay productive,

122
00:05:05,062 --> 00:05:07,122
remain useful until the very end.

123
00:05:08,109 --> 00:05:10,907
Maybe growing old gracefully also means knowing

124
00:05:10,921 --> 00:05:13,791
when I no longer have to prove any of those things.

125
00:05:15,476 --> 00:05:20,540
And perhaps there is one more thing I hope I can let go of. Regret.

126
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:27,102
My eighty-year-old self may look back at me today and say: You had enough money.

127
00:05:28,082 --> 00:05:31,548
Why didn't you stop working earlier and do what you really wanted?

128
00:05:32,528 --> 00:05:35,753
Or he may say: You needed the money.

129
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:38,721
Why didn't you work a few more years

130
00:05:38,736 --> 00:05:39,858
while you still could?

131
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:42,037
I don't know

132
00:05:42,051 --> 00:05:42,790
which old man

133
00:05:42,804 --> 00:05:43,628
I will become.

134
00:05:44,615 --> 00:05:45,993
I have to make decisions

135
00:05:46,007 --> 00:05:47,186
now without knowing how

136
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:48,649
the story turns out.

137
00:05:49,629 --> 00:05:50,766
Maybe that is what

138
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:52,442
makes regret so unfair.

139
00:05:53,422 --> 00:05:54,984
We judge our younger selves

140
00:05:54,999 --> 00:05:56,774
with information they never had.

141
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:01,684
So perhaps growing old gracefully is not reaching the end

142
00:06:01,699 --> 00:06:04,383
and discovering that I made all the right choices.

143
00:06:05,370 --> 00:06:07,274
Perhaps it is being able to look back

144
00:06:07,288 --> 00:06:09,305
at the person I used to be and say:

145
00:06:10,285 --> 00:06:11,691
You made the best choices you

146
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:13,197
could with what you knew then.

147
00:06:14,177 --> 00:06:15,441
Don't spend what's left

148
00:06:15,455 --> 00:06:17,231
regretting the choices you made.

149
00:06:18,211 --> 00:06:20,058
There isn't that much road ahead.

150
00:06:21,038 --> 00:06:23,069
Just live the last part of the journey.
